"Summarizes which participants communicate about related business topics while leaving message order and internal activities unspecified.\n\nUse when: Explain a large partner network at an overview level. Group many message exchanges into recognizable business conversations.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need the exact sequence, branching or content of individual messages.\n\nVisual grammar: Participant pools identify communication parties. Conversation hexagons group related message exchanges. Conversation links indicate participation and have no execution order.\n\nBuild: Choose the business collaboration and list its participants. Group related messages into short topic names such as payment. Connect each topic to its participants and expand complex topics separately.\n\nCheck: Every conversation includes the relevant communicating participants. Links mean participation rather than task sequence or ownership. Conversation topics correspond to real related message exchanges.\n\nPitfalls: Reading left-to-right placement as a timeline. Replacing an executable collaboration with unlabeled topic connections.\n\nExample: A marketplace overview groups buyer, merchant and payment provider exchanges into ordering, settlement and refund conversations.\n\nAlternatives: BPMN collaboration, Service ecosystem map."
